"A causal-order diagram represents local event order and message-induced precedence without inventing a shared global clock.\n\nUse when: Reason about concurrent events and distributed ordering. Explain Lamport timestamps or vector-clock comparisons.\n\nAvoid when: Measured wall-clock latency is the central question.\n\nVisual grammar: Lanes identify processes with locally ordered event markers. Message arrows connect sends to receives and establish causal precedence. Optional logical timestamps follow a declared clock algorithm.\n\nBuild: Choose a bounded event history and distinguish observation from example. Place each process’s events in local order. Connect matching sends and receives, then assign clock annotations if needed. Offset simultaneous-looking markers and route messages through clear inter-lane space.\n\nCheck: No receive precedes its corresponding send causally. Unconnected events are not declared concurrent without considering transitive paths. Lamport timestamp order is not treated as proof of causality. Clock labels and message paths remain unobstructed.\n\nPitfalls: Assuming horizontal alignment implies true simultaneity. Inferring physical elapsed time from logical timestamps.\n\nExample: Process A sends a change to B while C independently updates another record; the diagram preserves the partial order.\n\nAlternatives: Sequence diagram, Consensus protocol diagram."
